  he really he was fantastic very very  very down to earth and easy to work with  uh brought a great intensity to the role  he's got such strong presence and eyes  and and and focus any and i i feel like  he he doesn't need to try that hard to  kind of get across what we wanted with  stacker in this film which is a man  haunted by some guilt and by some  choices and and uh  and i just found him very believable and  and he was able to kind of bring a lot  of himself to that which is always nice  see you know it's always kind of what  you want as an actor to bring something  strong but not have to like force it and  work too hard and so  um i was really lucky in terms of those  four uh as a as a cast well there's  other there's other great actors  involved too but those were the kind of  the four bigger leads and then there's  janelle williams and hansa hack and some  other great actors too  uh yeah i found i you're you're totally  right his intensity in this  was great and it feels really pivotal to  pushing the story forward you know the  way in which  he's not gonna be  he's obviously found the direction that  he wants to go and he's not going to be  diverted by anybody not wanting him to  tell the truth  and i thought that was pretty beautiful  to witness  um  can you talk about the look of the film  um planning the look of the film with  your dp steve cozzins  yeah  i shot an earlier movie called born navy  blue a jazz bio pic a chet baker jazz  but i picked with steve  um and so we knew each other um  and steve has  is a great natural lighter and we  decided to shoot the film all handheld  steve operates himself um  and so we wanted to give it a like a  looser organic feel and we tried as much  as possible to use existing locations  without like we obviously production  design didn't set deca but we were  trying for quite a naturalistic look  because it's contemporary film  um and i thought steve did a wonderful  job of of of using natural light and not  it's not all natural lighting it's not  like extreme in that sense but  um and just giving us a certain  flexibility with with um with coverage  but also not trying to make it like it's  not like a frenetic fast-paced handheld  film it's still a pretty  quiet slow burn kind of feel but it's  handheld and often steve's handheld is  still very rock-solid so you don't  really want to notice it too much but  you still feel there's something  that's that's moving a little bit um and  then pallet wise i also didn't want to  kind of wash it out and detach it too  much i find like  that can become an overused trope now  and so i wanted to let the film have a  certain vibrancy  uh  and and color and and and i tend to  favor um  kind of wider lenses a lot of close-ups  you know are on some wider lenses so you  feel close to the characters emotionally  like you just as an audience feel close  to them so  um  yeah that was kind of those are some of  the basics of our of our approach to the  film  uh one of the things that i loved was uh  the choice of the song that lewis and  delia sing together ring them golden  bells which  i think we used to sing at my grandma's  church when i would go to church with my  grandma i think we sang this it was so  familiar with me for me yeah it's a  beautiful old song  the version that you chose  to go over the closing credits is by  southern suns but i noticed on spotify  there's hundreds of versions of this  song  why did you pick that one in particular  which i thought was great and i listened  to it several times  yeah i mean i thought the song was great  in that it's got this kind of hopeful  redemptive quality which at the end of  the day is kind of the end goal of  lewis's mission even though it gets dark  and violent and  um i think my editor jeff aschenhurst  even in the assembly had had it pulled  different versions of the song and he  had slapped it in there and then i i  really kind of fell in love with it  because it's it's it's kind of fun and  unique and and the way that some of the  old singers used to kind of harmonize  what they could do with just their  vocals is amazing yeah and so i kind of  i fell in love with that and then you  know to me it was like that song is is a  key motif in the film and then there's  the actual song delia is gone which was  originally by blind willie mctellin was  was covered by bob dylan and johnny cash  and i've always been a huge huge dylan  fan so i i came to this story and song  of delia's gone through bob dylan um  and i know that was also  one of the inspirations from michael  hamlin creating this and and if you look  at songs of that era like there's  delia's gone there's stackerly there's  um  uh i forget i'm i'm uh  i'm i'm blanking on the other one but  all the names of the characters like  louis delia stacker  um  billy lyons those are all characters  from folk songs of the 20s and so  there's this kind of like historic  americana folk song element  uh homage to all the characters and even  the story of of a young black girl being  raped is is what this song julius gone  is about it's based on it it was like  the original song was based on a true  story of a 14 year old black girl who  was raped and that but that you know  this is totally different but there's  still the spirit of that is still in  this story i think  so you've made uh have you made all of  your projects in canada  uh  yeah i mean i've shot i've shot them all  in canada for the most part i've shot  bits and bobs in other countries too but  they've kind of all been canadian based  and  often  use some canadian financing some  government support and then other you  know this typically having american  elements and canadian elements  i'm wondering if um  you've ever thought about the  comparisons but you know between being  an indie filmmaker trying to come up as  a filmmaker in canada versus the us we  ever thought about  i think some people have the perception  that's a bit easier in canada because of  funding for the arts but just wondering  what your thoughts are on that  uh i think in some ways it is easier  because we have more support  i mean it's still hard it's always still  hard to kind of like get the government  money here i know a lot of americans  come up and think it's just like part of  our tax credit you can just get all this  like free canadian money it's actually  really hard and competitive to get it  but  there is there is more opportunity to  get it and i think um what often me what  often happens is um  your your budgets can be a little bit  bigger than they would otherwise be like  i mean i i could make the same you know  my in in the us which is more market  driven you might have to make the same  movie for like two or three million  dollars you can make it for like five in  canada because  you have  30 or 40 or 50 of your budget covered by  some government subsidy and so that's i  think that's one of the differences is i  still i do feel quite lucky on that  front in that  we have the support but that being said  a lot of my movies are still pretty cast  driven which still means that to do them  on the level i i still need a certain  amount of um  they can't just be pure  government-supported movies they have to  have pretty strong american elements too  uh and or their  you know international co-productions a  lot of my past movies were canada  and europe or are just various  co-productions because then it allows  you more  cast flexibility because sometimes if  you're just making pure canadian movies  there's a lot of rules around  how many kittens you can cast how many  cans you can have and that can become a  little restricting um  and so  but i i think it is probably a little  easier it's a nice advantage to have  well so the film comes out  this friday  august 19th is that this friday already  oh perhaps that's next week  that's eight days  um it's soon though it's coming yeah  august 19th uh is it gonna be in  theaters and streaming or just theaters  in the beginning  this theaters is the plan in the in the  beginning and then uh  follow up on on streaming yeah  well congratulations on on a great film  uh we wish you the best of luck with it  and thank you so much for coming to talk  with us today  [Music]  [Laughter]  [Music]

